A||N||8||maninblack||tilt steering||||1||
Z||000000||maninblack||12-17-2001||09:28 PM||||I have tilt steering in my 87 se v6, recently<BR>the steering colume can still be move up and<BR>down,side to side.I checked the tilt steering level and it seems to be clicked into place.Anyone have this problem before,if<BR>so what is the fix.<BR>thx in advance||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rodrv6||12-17-2001||09:56 PM||||You have the wobbly column syndrome!<BR>This is very common with GM tilt columns.  4 bolts down inside the housing have loosened up. You can fix it, but it involves dissassembling the top part of the column, which can be a pain.  There are a couple of special tools that make it easier, but some folks just swap columns with one from a junkyard.  Check the Ogres Cave link at the bottom of the page for more info.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||maninblack||12-17-2001||10:30 PM||||thz, its even harder for me to find one in <BR>the yard here.I will give it a try anyway||||reg||1
Z||000003||GTDude||12-17-2001||11:11 PM||||It's really not all that hard once you've done it four or five hundred times and have the right tools.  I charge $150.00 to fix them and get it all day long.  Stay away from self fix.  You'll be so happy you did. LOL<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSCF0003a.JPG"> ||||reg||3
Z||000004||GT GT 87||12-17-2001||11:19 PM||||That collum is common in several GM cars , make sure you get one that matches your transmision. I bought one from Fiero Dave for $40 . They are a snap to change .It's cheaper to change than fix.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierokid87||12-18-2001||01:50 AM||||Yeah I had the same problem I changed mine to an 88 columb.  Easier then trying to fix it.  But i kept mine so that one day I can fix the old columb.  Scince it has the ignition that is for my Vin #.<P>Scott<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="red"> Fierokid's  Web site:</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="white">http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero</FONT><P><FONT COLOR="blue">AIM: Fierokid87<BR>ICQ: Fierokid</FONT><P>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sfiero.JPG">   <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierokid-xmas-sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||heavyhorse||12-18-2001||08:59 AM||||it's been a while but to the best of my recollection, on my 86 se, i pulled the steering wheel, removed the turn signal, horn contacts, etc, but not the turn signal stalk, and i was able to acces the four screws.  it does require a torx socket, ie female, rather then the male torx driver.  this was an E8 socket with a quarter inch drive so it could fit down in there.  took me only a couple of hours since i have had experience in pulling the steering wheel off before.  i should have put some blue loctite on the bolts but didn't.  this fixed mine right up.||||reg||1
Z||000007||maninblack||12-18-2001||09:57 AM||||thz for all the input, i will try to locate a<BR>use one first, its pretty rare here in vancouver canada.If i cant find one, i have no choice to pull the steering colume.<BR>thz||||reg||1
Z||000008||tesmith66||12-18-2001||01:22 PM||||It's easier and cheaper to fix it than to buy a used column that will come lose on you.<P>The special tools needed (steering wheel puller and lock plate compressor) can be rented for free from AutoZone. I use a harmonic balancer puller to get the wheel off and I have a lock plate compressor I bought from AutoZone for under $15.00.<P>Get a repair manual and follow the instructions. It's really easy, just time consuming. There are several little parts, so it's best to go slow, and take pictures if you don't think you can get it all back together.<P>If you need a new lock cylinder, now is a GREAT time to replace it. They are about $15.00 at the 'Zone.<P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
